    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d583">

                <pb facs="0823" n="743" sameAs="frus:frus1936v01/pg743"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">611.4731/141: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Consul General at
                        Sydney</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Moffat</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1936-03-06T15:00:00-05:00">March 6, 1936—3
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Your March 4, 5 p.m. Please inform Gullett that we have had constantly in mind
                    the Australian proposals of 1934<anchor n="3" xml:id="d583-f1"/> and have not
                    ceased to hope that we would eventually be able to negotiate on a reciprocal
                    basis. We regret to inform him, however, that conditions surrounding the
                    products in which Australia is interested have not changed to a point where we
                    could at this time make any undertakings in respect of duty reductions or
                    guaranteed quantities. Consequently, and in view also of Australia’s manifest
                    inability to offer concessions on our products, we fear we shall have to
                    postpone the suggested serious step towards negotiating an agreement.</p>
                <p>Please do not fail to enlarge upon the reference to ultimate benefits to
                    Australia from generalization which was made in our January 15, 8 p.m.,
                        1935,<anchor n="4" xml:id="d583-f2"/> and to point out that there is after
                    another year of our program even stronger ground for our asserted beliefs about
                    those benefits. It should not be overlooked that increased purchases by the
                    United States in other countries creates credits some of which are used to
                    purchase Australian products in increased quantities. Our program looks ahead to
                    normal multilateral trading which cannot be attained by the abortive bi-lateral
                    stop gaps now so prevalent in the world.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
